## What a `type: concept-scheme` instance is

A first-class representation of a TAG-VOCABULARY SCHEME — analog of `skos:ConceptScheme` from the SKOS standard.  A concept-scheme is a NAMED CONTAINER for a set of tags forming a coherent vocabulary (e.g., "memorial-types vocabulary", "library-domain vocabulary", "discipline-status vocabulary").  Tags become members of a scheme via `:mm.tag/in-scheme`, enabling vocabulary boundaries, scheme-scoped audits, and federation between vocabulary publishers.

## Why scheme membership matters

Without scheme containers, tags exist in a flat global namespace.  Conflicts ("foundation-substrate" tag in two unrelated contexts) become genuine ambiguity.  With schemes:

- **Vocabulary boundaries are explicit** — "this tag belongs to scheme X" disambiguates
- **Scheme-scoped queries** become natural — "show all tags in the memorial-types scheme"
- **Federation can target schemes** — a community vocabulary publisher exports a scheme; importers map it into their own scheme namespace
- **Auditing applies per-scheme** — closure-consistency, drift, orphan checks scope to scheme members

